"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[48448],{648448:e=>{e.exports="# Ingestion Diagnostics — Common Patterns\n\n## Guidelines\n\nKeep diagnostics strictly **read-only** toward the external source — credential validation, listing resources, schema inspection, and access verification are all appropriate. **Do not write data to, or modify the configuration of, the customer's external system** (no creates, updates, deletes, or configuration changes). If a fix requires changes on the external system, tell the user what to do there — do not make those changes via scripts or API calls.\n\n**Always** check for connector-specific **skills** and **documentation** for connector-specific issues, pitfalls, troubleshooting, and recommendations.\n\n## Connector-specific Guidance\n\n**Before answering any connector question, load the sub-skill file for the connector type.** These files contain required context — error codes, required permissions, prerequisites, and known failure modes that are not in this root skill:\n\n| Connector type                                   | File to load                                                   | Examples of what's inside                                                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Database (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle) | [`database-diagnostics.md`](database-diagnostics.md)           | CDC vs query-based modes, network paths, CDC permissions and source prerequisites, `INGESTION_GATEWAY_CDC_NOT_ENABLED` |\n| SaaS (Salesforce, HubSpot, ServiceNow, etc.)     | [`saas-diagnostics.md`](saas-diagnostics.md)                   | OAuth flows, API limits, object access                                                                                 |\n| Filesystem (SharePoint, OneDrive)                | [`filesystem-diagnostics.md`](filesystem-diagnostics.md)       | Permission models, site/drive access                                                                                   |\n| Cloud storage (S3, ADLS, GCS)                    | [`cloud-storage-diagnostics.md`](cloud-storage-diagnostics.md) | Bucket/container access, IAM, SAS tokens                                                                               |\n| Streaming                                        | [`streaming-diagnostics.md`](streaming-diagnostics.md)         | Broker connectivity, consumer group config                                                                             |\n\n## Diagnostic Flow\n\nDiagnosing issues with a pipeline generally follows these steps. Always check for more specific guidance from a skill for the connector itself — look for known pitfalls, troubleshooting steps, and recommendations there. The connection type on the connection determines which Lakeflow Connect connector is used.\n\n0. **Get Connection** - Get the connection name and any pipeline spec if there is already a pipeline created.\n1. **Validate credentials** — confirm the connection can authenticate to the source\n2. **Check source object access** — verify each configured table, report, or dataset is reachable\n3. **Inspect schema (optional)** — verify field access and structure\n4. **Suggest fix** — summarize what works, what doesn't, and what the user needs to change (credentials, permissions, object names, etc.)\n\n### Step 0 — Pipeline Context\n\nBefore diagnosing any connector issue, establish context.\n\n- Most critical is to get the **connection name**.\n- If a pipeline is already created, a pipeline spec started, or if the user knows what they want to ingest, get the: **source objects** (tables, reports, datasets)\n- If a pipeline was already attempted to run or be validated, get: **recent errors**.\n\n**First**, check whether this information is already available in your agent context. Use it directly — do not call tools to re-fetch information you already have. This is especially important before a pipeline has been created.\n\n**As a backup**, some agents expose tools to retrieve pipeline context. For example, the **Data Engineering agent on the Pipeline Details page** provides:\n\n- **`readPipelineDetails`** — returns the full pipeline spec including `ingestion_definition.connection_name` and source objects.\n- **`readPipelineIssues`** — returns recent errors and warnings from the latest pipeline update (error messages, dataset names, flow names, stack traces).\n  If these tools are not available in your current agent context, rely on whatever pipeline context was already provided.\n\nSelected objects in the pipeline spec vary by connector type:\n\n- `objects[].table.source_table` — table-based connectors (Salesforce, ServiceNow, HubSpot, etc.)\n- `objects[].schema` — schema-based connectors\n- `objects[].catalog.source_catalog` — catalog-based connectors (GA4)\n- `objects[].report` — report-based connectors (Workday RaaS)\n\n## Using Connector Tools\n\nIf the connector skill provides specific tools for accessing the external source directly, use them to support the diagnostic flow of validating credentials and checking access to the source and specific objects.\n\nSources with **>500K records** are high risk for timeout and performance issues. If `elapsed_seconds` in a tool response is high (connector-specific thresholds apply — e.g. >60s for GA4, >30 minutes for Workday), flag this to the user before they proceed.\n\n### Common HTTP Error Codes\n\nWhen calling connector diagnostic tools, interpret HTTP errors consistently:\n\n| Code | Meaning                        | Common causes                                                                  |\n| ---- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 401  | Invalid or expired credentials | Token expired, wrong password, refresh token revoked                           |\n| 403  | Permission denied              | Missing role, ACL restriction, IP allowlist blocking Databricks                |\n| 404  | Resource not found             | Table, report, or dataset name is wrong or was renamed                         |\n| 429  | Rate limit exceeded            | Too many requests; reduce sync frequency or number of selected objects         |\n| 5xx  | Server error                   | Check `elapsed_seconds` and the error message in the tool response for details |\n"}}]);
